    "data": {
      "A": "Build a low-risk serial candidate while retaining the existing spanning-tree search and deterministic traversal order. Target copies of graph/matrices/vectors, repeated map/queue allocation in cycle checks, scans of the spanning-tree edge list, and repeated color-set reconstruction; use reference passing, indexed scratch storage, cached tree adjacency, and incremental color counts where behavior is unchanged. Do not introduce heuristic pruning. Validate path/colour output and measure standalone against the 5.502 ms baseline.",
      "B": "Research an independent exact solver based on direct simple-path branch-and-bound rather than enumerating spanning trees. Track visited vertices and seen colours with dynamic bitsets, seed a valid incumbent cheaply, and use only admissible reachability/remaining-colour upper bounds. It may terminate early only on reaching K colours. Cover disconnected and sparse graphs, preserve valid output formatting, and verify optimality on small exhaustive cases; retain deterministic tie handling if the checker requires it.",
      "C": "Research a conditional 4-core parallel implementation of an exact search frontier, such as independent start vertices or first-edge branches. Eliminate reliance on the current global mutable search state: workers need local paths/incumbents, safe reduction, and cancellation only when the known upper bound K is reached. Keep a serial/small-work fallback because the measured baseline is only 5.502 ms and scheduling overhead may lose. Do not assume parallel work sharing improves correctness or speed; provide separate correctness and timing evidence.",
      "G": "Integrate only validated, compatible work from A/B/C. First check every result is a simple edge-valid path, reports its distinct-colour count, and is globally optimal for all valid inputs; account for any reference-output tie requirement. Benchmark each candidate and each proposed combination under the unchanged harness, with repeated samples, and keep the fastest valid final program. Do not blindly merge optimizations: an integration can regress from allocation, synchronization, altered traversal order, or startup cost.",
      "analysis": "The sole valid measurement is a 5.502 ms mean baseline on 4-core ARM64, so overhead and measurement noise matter. Source inspection shows substantial avoidable copying and repeated traversal work, but its spanning-tree enumeration is also a likely scalability bottleneck. The roles therefore separate safe serial reductions, an exact algorithmic alternative, and carefully gated parallelism. No candidate may specialize to observed tests, replace computation with expected output, or sacrifice correctness on valid inputs."
    },
